SD card longevity tuning for the OrangePi Zero 2W
=================================================

The Zero 2W boots from an SD card, which is the part most likely to wear out.
Everything in this board support package exists to keep writes off the card.
The board is expected to spend most of its life serving files (Samba) and
writing rarely.

Already handled by the vendor image
-----------------------------------
  * /etc/fstab mounts / with "noatime,commit=600,errors=remount-ro".
    noatime removes the per-read metadata write, commit=600 reduces the ext4
    journal commits to one every ten minutes.
  * /tmp is a tmpfs.
  * systemd-journald runs with Storage=volatile, i.e. the journal lives in RAM.

Added here
----------
  * etc/sysctl.d/90-sd-card.conf
      batches dirty writeback (60s), keeps caches, and makes the kernel use
      zram swap instead of dropping cache.
  * etc/default/zramswap
      compressed swap in RAM (50% of RAM, zstd, priority 100). The zram-tools
      package is installed by family_tweaks_s; the kernel needs CONFIG_ZRAM.
  * /var/log and /var/tmp are mounted as tmpfs (fstab entries added by
      family_tweaks_s), so logs never touch the card. Logs are therefore
      volatile: use "journalctl -b" while the system runs.
  * etc/tmpfiles.d/var-log.conf
      recreates the /var/log subdirectories (apt, journal, samba, private)
      after the tmpfs mount.
  * Masked systemd units
      apt-daily.timer, apt-daily-upgrade.timer, dpkg-db-backup.timer,
      e2scrub_all.timer, man-db.timer, logrotate.timer, rsyslog.service.
      The apt timers are the largest recurring writer on an otherwise idle
      system; rsyslog duplicates what the (volatile) journal already keeps.

Optional / manual
-----------------
  * Samba: keep "log level = 0" and the default "max log size"; with /var/log
    in RAM even verbose logging cannot wear the card.
  * Run "systemctl mask" on any additional timer you do not need.
  * To make logs persistent again, drop the /var/log line from /etc/fstab.
